WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:07.304
A mini-batch gradient is a noisy estimate of a larger-data objective’s gradient. Momentum combines the latest

00:00:07.304 --> 00:00:14.408
estimate with a state carried from earlier steps. This can sustain motion along persistent directions and

00:00:14.408 --> 00:00:15.740
smooth some oscillations.

00:00:16.267 --> 00:00:22.827
Different software can rescale the velocity. Here we use the unnormalized accumulated-gradient convention.

00:00:23.333 --> 00:00:28.473
The latest batch suggests one direction, while past gradients pull in the opposite direction.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:34.958
When will the old and new contributions exactly cancel? Pause here and make a prediction before

00:00:34.958 --> 00:00:35.330
continuing.

00:00:36.433 --> 00:00:44.163
A high momentum coefficient retains more of the previous velocity. The current batch does not necessarily

00:00:44.163 --> 00:00:51.892
determine the update’s direction by itself. For our starting inputs, Old velocity: 2. New batch gradient:

00:00:51.892 --> 00:00:55.273
-1. Combined velocity: 0.6. New weight: 0.94.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:02.253
At beta one-half the contributions cancel. Below it this example increases the weight; above it the

00:01:02.253 --> 00:01:03.060
weight decreases.

00:01:03.567 --> 00:01:09.487
Record optimizer conventions, batch sampling and learning-rate schedules when comparing training runs.

